{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In that day.</b> In those days." "<b>[He will punish].</b> This is the appointment written above." "<b>Leviathan.</b> It is the constellation and it is called 'fleeing' (בריח) because it runs from one end to the other." "<b>And upon Leviathan.</b> It too is in the sea, and it is in the form of a serpent, for there is also a serpent form in the sea, and עקלתון is an epithet." "<b>And the tannin</b> is the great one that is in the sea." "Some say that Leviathan the fleeing serpent and also עקלתון are on dry land, and the meaning concerns the kings of the earth, and the tannin is the king of Egypt, as it is written in the book of Ezekiel (Ezekiel 29:3). Some say the king of Tyre. And it seems right to Rabbi Moses the Kohen that it is an allusion, like 'and he destroyed every mighty man of valor, and leader, and prince' (2 Chronicles 32:21)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {5 "Resolved רמז marker as indicating the verse is read as an allusion; the syntax of the sentence is compressed and the comparison is to Chronicles as a parallel case of oblique royal language."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:55:20.526944531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}